# Routh II.Arts.157-159 — the invariable and instantaneous cones, rolling on
# one another — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "The Advanced Part of a Treatise on the Dynamics of a System of
# Rigid Bodies", Part II (Macmillan, 6th ed. 1905), Arts. 157-159. Art. 158
# names the two straight lines whose motion matters: "These are the invariable
# line and the instantaneous axis. The first of these is fixed in space, but as
# the body moves the invariable line [describes a cone in the body]."
#
# THE TWO CONES. Each line sweeps a cone, and the motion is one cone rolling on
# the other:
#
#   the SPACE cone  -- swept by the instantaneous axis about the invariable
#                      line, which is fixed in space. Half-angle = angle(w, L).
#   the BODY cone   -- swept by the instantaneous axis about the body's own
#                      figure axis. Half-angle = angle(w, figure axis).
#
# The body cone rolls on the space cone without slipping, their common
# generator at each instant being the instantaneous axis itself -- which is
# exactly why it is instantaneously at rest.
#
# WHEN THE CONES ARE CIRCULAR. For a general asymmetric body neither cone is
# circular and neither half-angle is constant. For a UNIAXAL body (A = B) both
# are exactly circular and both half-angles are rigorously constant, which is
# what makes the rolling picture literal rather than approximate. This notebook
# therefore uses a uniaxal body, and prints both half-angles.
#
# THE NUMBERS. A cuboid of mass 3 with half-extents (1, 1, 2) gives
# I = diag(5, 5, 2), so A = B = 5, C = 2, figure axis z. Started at
# w = (0.6, 0, 3):
#
#     L = (3, 0, 6)                    G = 6.708203932499369
#     |w| = 3.059411708155671          (constant, because A = B)
#
#     space cone:  cos(angle(w, L))      = 0.9647638212377322   (15.29 deg)
#     body cone:   cos(angle(w, figure)) = 3 / |w| = 0.9805806756909202  (11.32 deg)
#
# Both are printed repeatedly below and neither moves. The body-cone angle is
# read from the body-frame angular velocity, whose z-component stays at exactly
# 3 while its transverse part rotates -- that is the cone.
#
# SETUP NOTE. system.g_constant = 0 so nothing else acts.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: a square-section box in steady precession. Two cones are
# rolling on each other inside that motion, one fixed in space and one fixed in
# the body, touching along the axis the body is momentarily turning about.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p2_rolling_cones
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_rolling_cones.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_rolling_cones.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [1, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [0.6, 0, 3] }

get obj0.inertia_tensor
norm(obj0.angular_momentum)
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)
# space-cone half-angle: cos(angle between w and the invariable line)
dot(obj0.angular_velocity, obj0.angular_momentum) / (norm(obj0.angular_velocity) * norm(obj0.angular_momentum))
# body-frame w -- its z-component over |w| is the body-cone half-angle
[obj0.orientation.w, 0 - obj0.orientation.x, 0 - obj0.orientation.y, 0 - obj0.orientation.z] * [0, obj0.angular_velocity.x, obj0.angular_velocity.y, obj0.angular_velocity.z] * obj0.orientation

run 1.2 steps 2
dot(obj0.angular_velocity, obj0.angular_momentum) / (norm(obj0.angular_velocity) * norm(obj0.angular_momentum))
[obj0.orientation.w, 0 - obj0.orientation.x, 0 - obj0.orientation.y, 0 - obj0.orientation.z] * [0, obj0.angular_velocity.x, obj0.angular_velocity.y, obj0.angular_velocity.z] * obj0.orientation
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)

run 1.2 steps 2
dot(obj0.angular_velocity, obj0.angular_momentum) / (norm(obj0.angular_velocity) * norm(obj0.angular_momentum))
[obj0.orientation.w, 0 - obj0.orientation.x, 0 - obj0.orientation.y, 0 - obj0.orientation.z] * [0, obj0.angular_velocity.x, obj0.angular_velocity.y, obj0.angular_velocity.z] * obj0.orientation
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [1, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [0.6, 0, 3] }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.02
